**assistant**:
> What’s happening is that you’ve accidentally nested **two Bootstrap pages** inside each other, and that breaks the JavaScript that shinyWidgets uses for `pickerInput`.
> 
> ### The core issue
> 
> You have:
> 
> ```r
> ui <- page_navbar(
>   # ...
>   nav_panel(
>     title = "Reports and Datasets",
>     fluidPage(                  # <-- inner Bootstrap page
>       uiOutput("outputsTable")
>     )
>   ),
>   nav_panel(
>     title = "Climate Risks",
>     fluidPage(                  # <-- another inner page
>       # ... lots of UI, including pickerInput ...
>     )
>   ),
>   # ...
> )
> ```
> 
> `page_navbar()` already *is* a full-page layout with its own `<body>`, container, rows, etc. When you put `fluidPage()` *inside* a `nav_panel()`, you’re effectively creating a nested Bootstrap layout. That:
> 
> - Re-initializes some Bootstrap markup and classes inside the tab content,
> - Can confuse how dropdowns and input bindings are attached,
> - And in practice, often breaks components that rely on specific container structure—like `pickerInput`’s dropdown.
> 
> When you comment out the inner `fluidPage()`:
> 
> ```r
> nav_panel(
>   title = "Reports and Datasets",
>   ##fluidPage(
>     uiOutput("outputsTable")
>   ##)
> ),
> ```
> 
> the tab content becomes just “normal” UI inside the already-correct page structure, and the pickers in other tabs start working again.
> 
> Even though the problem is *visible* on the second tab, it’s the markup created in the first tab that’s corrupting the overall DOM / Bootstrap environment.
> 
> ### What you should do
> 
> 1. **Remove `fluidPage()` inside each `nav_panel`**. Just put the contents directly:
> 
> ```r
> ui <- page_navbar(
>   title="PROSPER Adaptation Investment Support",
>   selected="Climate Risks",
>   bg = "#2D89C8",
>   inverse = TRUE,
> 
>   nav_panel(
>     title = "Reports and Datasets",
>     uiOutput("outputsTable")
>   ),
> 
>   nav_panel(
>     title = "Climate Risks",
>     tags$style(type = "text/css", " ... "),
>     tags$script(HTML("$(function() { $('#map1').css('max-width', '100%'); });")),
>     titlePanel(h1("UK Climate Change Risk Maps", style='text-align: center')),
>     sidebarLayout(
>       sidebarPanel(
>         # ...
>         pickerInput(...),
>         pickerInput(...)
>       ),
>       mainPanel(
>         # ...
>       )
>     )
>   ),
> 
>   nav_panel(
>     title="Adaptation Costs & Benefits",
>     useShinyjs(),
>     tags$head(tags$script(HTML(JS.logify))),
>     tags$head(tags$script(HTML(JS.onload))),
>     titlePanel(h1("Adaptation Costs & Benefits", style='text-align: center')),
>     sidebarLayout(
>       sidebarPanel(
>         pickerInput(...),
>         pickerInput(...),
>         sliderInput(...)
>       ),
>       mainPanel(
>         # ...
>       )
>     )
>   ),
> 
>   # nav_item, nav_spacer, nav_menu...
> )
> ```
> 
> 2. Don’t wrap `nav_panel` contents in `fluidPage`, `navbarPage`, etc. The `page_*` family from bslib (`page_navbar`, `page_fluid`, etc.) is the **top-level** layout; you then put normal components (like `sidebarLayout`, `fluidRow`, `column`, etc.) directly inside panels.
> 
> Once you de-nest those inner `fluidPage()` calls, you should have:
> 
> - `pickerInput` opening correctly on both tabs,
> - No need for hacks around that first tab.
> 
> If after this change you still see odd behavior, we can then look at Bootstrap versions (`bs_theme(version = 3 or 5)`), but your “commenting out fluidPage fixes it” observation is a very clean indicator that nested pages are the culprit.
