> `page_navbar()` + `nav_panel()` already give you a full “page section” layout, and `titlePanel()` is designed for the classic `fluidPage()` layout. When you remove the inner `fluidPage()`, `titlePanel()`’s default Bootstrap spacing looks oversized/awkward in the new context.
> 
> You can fix this in one of three ways:
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 1. Replace `titlePanel()` with a simpler heading
> 
> Since you don’t need the old `titlePanel` layout, just use a header tag with a small margin. For example:
> 
> ```r
> nav_panel(
>   title = "Climate Risks",
>   tags$style(type = "text/css", " ... "),
>   tags$script(HTML("$(function() { $('#map1').css('max-width', '100%'); });")),
>   
>   tags$div(
>     style = "text-align: center; margin: 10px 0 5px 0;",
>     tags$h1("UK Climate Change Risk Maps", style = "margin: 0;")
>   ),
> 
>   sidebarLayout(
>     sidebarPanel(
>       # ...
>     ),
>     mainPanel(
>       # ...
>     )
>   )
> )
> ```
> 
> Similarly in the Adaptation tab:
> 
> ```r
> nav_panel(
>   title = "Adaptation Costs & Benefits",
>   useShinyjs(),
>   tags$head(tags$script(HTML(JS.logify))),
>   tags$head(tags$script(HTML(JS.onload))),
> 
>   tags$div(
>     style = "text-align: center; margin: 10px 0 5px 0;",
>     tags$h1("Adaptation Costs & Benefits", style = "margin: 0;")
>   ),
> 
>   sidebarLayout(
>     # ...
>   )
> )
> ```
> 
> That gives you tight, controlled spacing and keeps everything inside `page_navbar` well-behaved.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 2. Keep `titlePanel()` but override its margins via CSS
> 
> If you like using `titlePanel()` semantically, you can shrink the extra whitespace globally:
> 
> ```r
> nav_panel(
>   title = "Climate Risks",
>   tags$style(HTML("
>     .tab-pane .page-header {
>       margin-top: 10px;
>       margin-bottom: 5px;
>     }
>     .tab-pane .page-header h1 {
>       margin-top: 0;
>       margin-bottom: 0;
>       text-align: center;
>     }
>   ")),
> 
>   titlePanel(h1("UK Climate Change Risk Maps")),
>   sidebarLayout(
>     # ...
>   )
> )
> ```
> 
> This reuses the existing `page-header` class but tightens it.
> 
> ---
> 
> ## 3. Use bslib’s more “native” page header style
> 
> If you want to lean into bslib conventions:
> 
> ```r
> nav_panel(
>   title = "Climate Risks",
>   bslib::card(
>     full_screen = FALSE,
>     style = "margin-bottom: 10px;",
>     bslib::card_body(
>       tags$h1("UK Climate Change Risk Maps", style = "margin: 0; text-align: center;")
>     )
>   ),
>   sidebarLayout(
>     # ...
>   )
> )
> ```
> 
> Slightly heavier markup, but consistent with bslib’s newer components.
> 
> ---
> 
> For minimal change and good aesthetics, I’d go with **Option 1**: replace `titlePanel()` with a simple `div + h1` and control the margins manually. That keeps your pickerInputs working (no nested `fluidPage`) and restores a clean, compact title appearance.
